    scope("Original Culture prose continuity", "Idiran imperial order during the Idiran-Culture War, before later machine emancipation", "Biological Idirans and their subordinate populations; a nonhuman political proxy", ["Later Culturized communities", "A point that averages the war and postwar settlement", "Only the privileged warrior population"], True),
    "The Idiran Empire is retained as a zero-variant reference because its religious order rejects true machine autonomy despite possessing advanced technology. Political and military purposes remain those of biological rulers. This is a useful contrast with the Culture, where independent machine citizens are central to the social system. The control point is therefore near concentrated biological command rather than autonomous AI rule.\n\nThat contrast does not establish the empire's complete welfare arrangements. Its subordinate populations also prevent using an image of privileged warriors as a guarantee to everyone. Both the guarantee coordinate and the nonworking fraction remain unresolved rather than being assigned from the empire's reputation for conquest. The snapshot stops before the later changes to Idiran computer systems. A direct review of the novel's appendix is still needed to strengthen the current secondary evidence and decide whether that later order merits a separate candidate.",
