    scope("The Algebraist; not the Culture setting", "Mercatorial order before the novel's ending changes its strategic position", "Human and other biological inhabitants of the Mercatoria", ["Dweller Common entitlements", "Hypothetical reforms after the ending", "All imperial worlds assumed economically identical"], True),
    "The Mercatoria's persecution of machine intelligence makes it a zero variant with a direct technological basis. Capable AIs exist in the setting, but the imperial order seeks to prevent their open existence and authority. Production and government therefore cannot simply be described as an independent machine welfare system. The control placement remains near a concentrated biological hierarchy.\n\nThe ban is much better supported than any universal account of income or basic provision across the empire. The Dwellers' Common system belongs to a different society and is not transferred to the Mercatoria merely because both appear in one book. The guarantee and nonworking axes are left open. The novel's ending also permits hopes of political change without describing a fully enacted new economy. Such possibilities are research questions, not additional completed regimes. This entry separates an established policy toward AI from claims about everyday distribution that require further primary passages.",
